WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.290
SPEAKER 1: Matthew Mehan is the
Director of Academic Programs

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.110
and the Worsham Teaching Fellow
at Hillsdale College's Kirby

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.270
Center in Washington, DC.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:26.250
He earned his PhD at the
University of Dallas,

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.550
where he wrote a dissertation
on Shakespeare's vision

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.000
for leadership education in
his collaborative play Sir

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.320
Thomas More.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.080
Dr. Mehan has been a Publius
Fellow at the Claremont

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.080
Institute, and is a Fellow
of the Center for Thomas More

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.020
Studies, and a reader
at the Folger Library.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:44.240
He has served as a
speechwriter and communications

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.280
consultant for civic
leaders around the country.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.660
He is the author of Mr.
Mehan's Mildly Amusing Mythical

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.540
Mammals, an illustrated
book of poems.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:58.115
Please join me in welcoming
Dr. Matthew Mehan.

00:00:58.115 --> 00:01:01.510
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:08.330
MATTHEW MEHAN:
Thank you very much.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:10.420
Applied mathematics,
it's awesome.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.870
I hear it's a lit talk.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:15.400
I guess Matt Bell has a sense
of humor to pair us together,

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:16.190
but that's good.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.200
That's the liberal arts.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.460
Thank you all for coming.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.280
Thank you, Matt Bell, for
shepherding me out here.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.170
And thank you all
for your support

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:31.260
of Hillsdale, particularly
our new venture,

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.580
which I've been a part
of out in Washington, DC,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:38.090
the Steve and Amy Van Andel
Graduate School of Government.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:42.890
I got the unbelievable honor to
teach the very first classes,

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:47.660
very first classes, very
first class this fall.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.650
And it was just wonderful.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:54.050
We started with Antigone,
and the Book Of Proverbs,

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.760
and Aristotle's
Nicomachean Ethics.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.270
And it's happening
right on Capitol Hill.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.400
And it's been a really
great honor in my life.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.165
So thank you all for
supporting Hillsdale.

00:02:05.165 --> 00:02:07.490
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.540
Before I speak directly
about Thomas More's Utopia,

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:19.430
I'd like to start with three
jokes about Marxist socialists,

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:21.040
but I repeat myself.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.370
From the satirical humor
site McSweeney's, usually

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.580
left leaning, but nevertheless--

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.430
see if you can spot what
all three of the jokes

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:33.600
have in common.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.220
The first one-- knock, knock.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.410
Who's there?

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:38.300
A Marxist socialist.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.460
A Marxist socialist who?

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:44.175
A Marxist socialist who wants to
give you a pamphlet about class

00:02:44.175 --> 00:02:44.675
struggle.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:48.766
I'll try another one, sorry.

00:02:48.766 --> 00:02:50.510
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:54.290
What did one Marxist
socialist say to another?

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:57.290
Like you, I also advocate
a proletarian revolution

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:01.310
culminating in
collective ownership.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.590
Last one, bear with me.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:07.580
What do you get when you cross
a Marxist with a socialist?

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.600
Two people who generally feel
that the value of a commodity

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.190
is equal to its socially
necessary labor time.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:18.510
They're great fun at parties.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:19.890
I think that's
the takeaway here.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.570
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:27.000
What the humorist at
McSweeney's is getting at

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.220
is what the famous German
socialist Friedrich

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.840
Engels knew as a distinct
trait of German socialists,

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:36.450
where all this sort of began.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:40.020
In the introduction to his
English edition of Socialism--

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:43.440
Utopian and Scientific-- and
there he's borrowing from

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.960
the word coined by
Thomas More there--

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.650
which is a collected series
of-- shocker-- pamphlets--

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.560
by the way, they're
great fun at parties.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.150
They give you pamphlets.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:57.840
Poor Friedrich seems defensive
before his witty English

00:03:57.840 --> 00:03:59.520
audience when he writes this.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:05.250
"As is well known, we Germans
are of a terribly ponderous

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.030
grundlichkeit."

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:08.010
OK.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:11.250
Radical profundity, or
profound radicality,

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.530
whatever you may
like to call it.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:16.380
When you learn
that grundlichkeit

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.720
basically means thoroughness--

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.640
that's all it means--

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.670
the next line becomes
unintentionally hilarious.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.740
Engels continues to
expound thoroughly

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:27.510
on the meaning of
thoroughness this way--

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:30.270
"Whenever any one
of us expounds what

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.910
he considers a new
doctrine, he has first

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:36.720
to elaborate it into an
all-compromising system.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.953
He has to prove that both
the principles of logic

00:04:38.953 --> 00:04:40.620
and the fundamental
laws of the universe

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.410
had existed from all
eternity for no other purpose

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.060
than to ultimately
lead to this newly

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.610
discovered crowning theory."

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.350
And it gets even
more ridiculous after

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:54.000
that when this father of modern
German socialism we find out

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.060
was trying to satirize
another socialist.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.010
And after going through
another page of this,

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:02.730
he says finally, with a little
grundlichkeit of his own,

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:05.490
"Anyhow, the systematic
comprehensiveness

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:07.410
of my opponent gave
me the opportunity

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.560
of developing in opposition
to him and in a more connected

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.200
form than had
previously been done

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.480
the views held by
Karl Marx and myself

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:18.990
on this great
variety of subjects."

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.990
Like I said, Thanksgiving
dinner just isn't the same

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:25.950
without Uncle Friedrich arguing
with good old cousin Mr.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:28.800
Professor Dr. E. Duhring.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.960
This is how they speak.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.820
They speak heavy, ponderous
monologues, massive systems.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:38.400
It's not a witty group.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.450
In short, German socialists
are not known for their wit.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.850
And Friedrich Engels
knew it well enough

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:48.330
to be sheepish about it before
the witty British audience he

00:05:48.330 --> 00:05:50.730
was seeking to
persuade in that essay.

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:52.710
Without arguing here,
permit me to assert

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:57.570
that a people tend to be
witty if they've been led so

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:01.170
by the witty examples of their
famous writers, speakers,

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:04.050
their poets, historians,
and statesmen,

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:07.620
and if they're very fortunate,
even their philosophers.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.900
Not incidental to our
purpose today, Thomas More

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.820
was all of these things.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:17.310
But let me be more thorough and
give you the testimony of one

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:20.070
of More's own contemporaries.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:23.490
"Thomas More is a man of
an angel's wit and singular

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:24.420
learning.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:26.880
He is a man of many
excellent virtues.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.160
I know not his
fellow, for where is

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.620
the man in whom
is so many goodly

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:36.030
virtues, of that gentleness,
lowliness, and affability,

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:39.180
and as time requires, a
man of marvelous mirth,

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.990
and pastimes, and sometimes
of steadfast gravity,

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:45.630
a man for all seasons."

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:47.640
Those were the words of
Robert Whittington, who

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:50.970
was a liberal arts teacher
and translator of Cicero

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:54.540
and Seneca, two Roman
philosopher statesman orders

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:57.360
deeply favored by
Thomas More and featured

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:01.590
in More's most famous and
famously misunderstood Utopia.

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:05.310
The phrase "man for all
seasons" suits more well,

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:08.280
so well that it became the
hallmark of the 20th century

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.170
understanding of him.

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:12.120
In his lifetime,
More was speaker

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:13.498
of the House of Commons.

00:07:13.498 --> 00:07:15.540
And while he was speaker
of the House of Commons,

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:19.530
he secured the first
right of free speech--

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:21.940
not for the whole of London
or the whole of England,

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:23.460
but just for the
Parliament so they

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:28.230
could speak freely to the king
without risking their lives.

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:30.720
He was also the Lord Chancellor
of England, stepping up,

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.862
as Shakespeare put it, up nearly
to his country's head, right

00:07:33.862 --> 00:07:35.820
below the King, essentially
effectively running

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.720
the government for him.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.170
And he was a famous poet,
historian, and essayist,

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:43.300
as well as a brilliant lawyer,
and an attentive father.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:44.880
And after his
execution and death,

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.440
More was praised before
Queen Elizabeth--

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:50.850
that's Henry VIII's
father, whom he opposed--

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:52.770
and the entire
Parliament at St. Paul's

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.530
Cathedral in Shakespeare's
time, and named

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:58.650
a saint of both the Anglican
and Catholic churches

00:07:58.650 --> 00:07:59.950
in our own time.

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:02.615
That, to me, is sort of
the great combo trick.

00:08:02.615 --> 00:08:04.740
I don't know how you do
that, the Church of England

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.750
and the Roman Church.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:10.350
But anyway, all of these
impressive achievements

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:16.410
of More aside, in 1954 the
playwright Robert Bolt wrote

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:18.180
A Man For All
Seasons, about More's

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:21.510
artful and courageous resistance
to King Henry VIII, who

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:24.720
violated the Magna Carta
and his coronation oath

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.580
in order to divorce
his wife and declare

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:29.310
himself the head of
both church and state.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:33.510
More's refusal to accept this
Caesar opapism of King Henry

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:35.940
landed More in the
Tower of London and then

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.590
on the chopping block.

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:41.309
Likewise, it is that
same stalwart objection

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:45.150
by More to the combination
of state and church authority

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:47.460
that sent the Puritan
pilgrims to Plymouth

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:50.370
and the Catholic
pilgrims to Maryland.

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:52.260
The film version of
Man For All Seasons

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:55.410
won eight Academy
Awards in 1967,

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.180
including Best
Picture and Director.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:00.030
And in 1990, the Law
Society of Great Britain

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:02.730
named him Lawyer
of the Millennium.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.850
And Pope John Paul II named
him the Patron of Statesmen

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:08.710
for the Catholic
Church worldwide.

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:11.070
Despite all these
various accolades

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:13.620
and honors over the
centuries, despite being

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:16.740
a principal source of a
number of Shakespeare's plays,

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.710
despite being considered by
Jonathan Swift to be, quote,

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.260
"the person of the
greatest virtue

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:26.700
these islands ever produced,"
it is nevertheless true

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:29.160
what Robert Bolt
More's character

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:31.860
says to a simple-minded
Spaniard diplomat

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:34.350
when he presumes to
know what More thinks

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:36.310
about the politics of the day.

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:38.310
The Spaniard-- (SPANISH
ACCENT) "But your views,

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:40.890
Sir Thomas More,
are well known,"

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:45.720
which More contradicts, saying,
"My views are much guessed at."

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:49.020
So too today, with careless,
unhistorical, and confusing

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:54.020
melodramas like HBO's The
Tudors, and BBC's Wolf Hall,

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:57.900
More's view remains much
guessed at, but still

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.400
very poorly known.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:01.890
And that's especially
true when it

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:05.610
comes to his most complex
and confounding ironic work,

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:10.320
Utopia, written 1560, published
throughout the continent,

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:13.830
and England, and wildly
popular from the start.

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:18.030
I mean, it was the book
of Europe at the time.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:20.247
We have Hunger Games,
they had Utopia,

00:10:20.247 --> 00:10:21.330
sort of a different world.

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:22.810
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:26.370
More makes himself a character
in the dialogue of Utopia,

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:30.090
along with his friend and fellow
Christian humanist Peter Giles.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:34.860
But it's a tricky dialogue that
has within it both a monologue

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:37.860
about a different dialogue
from before the dialogue

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:39.960
and a different monologue
within the dialogue

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.840
that's longer than the rest
of the entire dialogue,

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:44.850
except that the monologue
is technically a very

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:47.837
long part of the dialogue.

00:10:47.837 --> 00:10:49.420
That's a little
grundlichkeit for you.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:51.210
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:54.210
The two major
monologues in Utopia

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:56.010
are given by the
third interlocutor

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:59.940
in the dialogue, a strange
and passionate character.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:04.410
He's a traveler and scholar
named Raphael Hythloday,

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:08.640
who claims to have lived on
the amazing island of Utopia--

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:11.410
which means no
place, by the way--

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:14.010
and with its novel
and he says, "truly

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:16.950
excellent political
institutions."

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:19.980
The text of Utopia is
divided into two books,

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:23.100
and Raphael Hythloday
dominates both of them.

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:26.160
Book two of Utopia
is almost exclusively

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:29.100
Raphael's long and
extremely thorough account

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:33.120
of the laws, habits, and
customs of the Utopians.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:35.160
And even though
book two technically

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:38.340
has More's character
speak at the very end,

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:41.100
it is really Raphael
Hythloday's monologue,

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.900
with the other speakers
reduced to mere onlookers.

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:47.520
Hythloday frustrates
any authentic dialogue

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:49.080
at that point.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:50.880
And I'm kind of
reminded of the bad guy

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:52.325
syndrome from The Incredibles.

00:11:52.325 --> 00:11:53.790
You remember that line?

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:55.840
"You sly dog, you
caught me monologuing."

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:58.650
The bad guys in the comic
books are always monologuing

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:00.527
about their evil plans.

00:12:00.527 --> 00:12:02.610
There's something about
that in Raphael Hythloday.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.820
He just gets going,
and you can't stop him.

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:09.820
This chronic monologuer's name
means both healer of God--

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:11.880
that's Raphael, Rafael--

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:15.210
and skilled speaker
of nonsense--

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:17.940
hythlodaeus in Greek.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:19.830
By giving the character
a name like that,

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:22.680
More sets the attentive
reader on guard

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:25.410
to suspect that whatever
Raphael Hythloday is going

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:30.150
to say about this island and
its institutions, Utopia,

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:34.180
you can bet it's going to be a
mixture of sense and nonsense.

00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:35.370
And so it is.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:38.700
But you'd need to have the
wit to see that More is not

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:42.930
being ponderous, literal,
and radical, but rather

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:46.110
witty, ironic, and subtle.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:49.770
To understand More's
Utopia properly,

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:54.240
the habit of socialist, Marxist,
communist grundlichkeit--

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:55.860
but, again, I repeat myself--

00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:57.870
is not very helpful.

00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:01.590
Because that cast of mind
and cast of character

00:13:01.590 --> 00:13:05.010
only stifles the wit
required to see well

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:08.640
the practical, political,
and actual world

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:10.897
in which we live and work.

00:13:10.897 --> 00:13:13.230
Now, I want to take a moment
to apologize for this talk,

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:18.510
because it combines a few
things that don't mix very well.

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:21.400
Presenting a talk on both
heavy and sad socialism,

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:23.760
it's a very grim, dark thing.

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:25.590
Matt Bell's talking about hell.

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:28.080
He sort of set that up for us.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:31.560
And then we've got to combine
it with More's witty and wise,

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:33.160
mirthful Utopia.

00:13:33.160 --> 00:13:36.210
It's like serving a peanut
butter sandwich mixed

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:41.100
with pickled herring or
making a Netflix special

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:43.440
featuring the art of
laughter-- the joy

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:46.375
of laughter and oral surgery.

00:13:46.375 --> 00:13:47.265
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:51.840
Or it's as if you dressed up for
Halloween as someone pretending

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:52.650
to be you.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.600
What?

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.360
If you're puzzled at that sort
of ironic and contradictory

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:02.460
humor, where you thought
it was going one way

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:06.100
and it goes another, welcome
to Thomas More's Utopia.

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:10.590
It's a very puzzling book that
gives you the distinct sense

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:12.570
that you are not
always in on the joke,

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:16.320
at least not at first, but that
the humor is, nevertheless,

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:19.620
there to help the reader
to more than just laughs.

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:23.940
In other words, its ironic
jokes are not very socialist.

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:26.190
Because, as we all
know, socialist jokes

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:28.830
are only funny if
everybody gets it.

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:30.570
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:31.320
Wait for it.

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:31.820
No.

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:33.270
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:36.180
In fact, Utopia is
so puzzling that it's

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:39.450
been famously nicknamed
the statesman's puzzle.

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:41.790
Yet the book is less
and less puzzling

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:43.700
the wittier and more
ironically humorous

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:46.150
a lens you use to read it.

00:14:46.150 --> 00:14:48.150
On the other hand,
Utopia is far more

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:53.010
puzzling for people who have a
bad case of grundlichkeit, that

00:14:53.010 --> 00:14:57.720
ponderous seriousness that
can't handle dialogue and double

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.190
meaning.

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:01.650
The great critic of
socialism and communism,

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:04.320
Alexander Solzhenitsyn,
put it this way--

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:07.410
"No one would ever
dare accuse Stalin

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:09.047
of having a sense of humor."

00:15:09.047 --> 00:15:10.450
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:13.290
They're not known for
their sense of humor.

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:15.990
With 50 million dead, I
suppose that joke is always

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:22.920
sort of #TooSoon, but they're
dark, deep, serious, grim

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:24.840
fellows.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:28.980
And More has even famously--

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:33.550
was even famously witty
in the hour of his death.

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:37.650
So here's the difference
between Stalin and More.

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:41.160
Unlike the brutal, humorous,
unfunny, socialist, communist

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:42.570
Stalin--

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:44.100
again, I repeat myself--

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:46.800
who murdered many millions,
the Christian, humanist, poet,

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:50.220
lawyer, statesman
loved to laugh.

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:52.650
He referred to himself
as having the nature

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:55.460
of half a giglet and more.

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:57.450
Do you guys know
what a giglet is?

00:15:57.450 --> 00:16:00.300
If you ever go to
Italy or Mexico,

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:03.720
the street vendors will carve
these little wooden dentures.

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:07.050
And the way you open them, they
scrape, and they go wa, wa, wa.

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:09.362
So, it's like a little
laughing denture.

00:16:09.362 --> 00:16:11.570
But that's how More referred
to himself in his books,

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:13.620
is I can't stop
laughing, even though I'm

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:15.030
this very serious statesman.

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:18.480
Please don't tickle
my funny bone.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:21.380
In his lifetime, More was
famous for using humor,

00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:23.390
both to mock his opponents--

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:25.400
not always with
sarcasm, mind you--

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:28.070
but very often also
with witty irony

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:32.420
that was difficult to defeat
and that pricked the conscience.

00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:34.490
When Sir More rose
to the national stage

00:16:34.490 --> 00:16:38.240
as speaker of the House, he
was known as Master Mock.

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:39.650
And More was even
famously witty,

00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:42.590
like I said, on the scaffold.

00:16:42.590 --> 00:16:45.620
Among other jests, when
rising to the scaffold

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:49.130
and the chopping block, he
joked to his own headsman,

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:52.760
"I pray you, let me lay
my beard over the block,

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:54.420
lest you should cut it.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:56.660
The reason being, of course,
that my beard has not

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:58.775
offended the king."

00:16:58.775 --> 00:17:01.880
This is the moment of death.

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:04.849
More mentioned-- not a
few times, by the way--

00:17:04.849 --> 00:17:07.490
in his writings, in his
letters, that his last name

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:11.000
in both Latin and
Greek means fool.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:16.369
And his first name, Didymus,
Thomas, means twin or double.

00:17:16.369 --> 00:17:18.980
Also, it means mulberry,
but that's really neither

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:21.530
here nor there.

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:24.260
After More's death in
the times that followed,

00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:27.770
he was honored by Shakespeare
with a very favorable

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:30.290
collaborative play
called Sir Thomas More--

00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:33.060
you heard I did my
dissertation on that play--

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:36.740
which Shakespeare co-wrote with
five other popular playwrights

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:37.810
of his day.

00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:39.690
So imagine if Spielberg, Nolan--

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:45.620
pick your top-- Lucas--

00:17:45.620 --> 00:17:48.140
big, big names-- Whedon--

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:50.180
all got together
and said, OK, we

00:17:50.180 --> 00:17:52.240
are going to produce this play.

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:56.750
The censors said,
no, you may not,

00:17:56.750 --> 00:17:58.310
because it had a
riot scene, and they

00:17:58.310 --> 00:18:01.670
didn't want anyone thinking
about rioting in London.

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:04.600
But it was, nevertheless,
a professionally produced--

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:07.382
or written play by some
of the top playwrights.

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:13.670
More-- I lost my place here.

00:18:13.670 --> 00:18:15.410
In it, true to
biographical accounts,

00:18:15.410 --> 00:18:18.920
More is completely
serious when he must be--

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:20.740
Quelling a citywide
riot in London

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:23.240
and administering justice for
the realm at the privy council

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:24.080
of the king.

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:27.020
But otherwise he's constantly
dropping witty mocks,

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:28.910
funny puns, and clever
and ironic jokes

00:18:28.910 --> 00:18:33.140
for his friends, Desiderius
Erasmus and even very subtly

00:18:33.140 --> 00:18:35.570
for his friend King
Henry VIII, always

00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:38.860
with an eye to both their
delight and their instruction.

00:18:38.860 --> 00:18:41.270
More even stages a humble
little morality play

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:44.930
within the play for his friend
the Lord Mayor of London,

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:48.320
called the Marriage
of Wit and Wisdom.

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:51.440
Indeed throughout the play, More
jests in his witty, wise way,

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:53.840
staging humorous
plays of his own

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:56.480
that contain high moral matter.

00:18:56.480 --> 00:18:58.580
For instance, when
imprisoned in the tower

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:01.440
on the eve of execution,
he tells the guard

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:04.790
a low potty humor joke that
has a high and subtle lesson

00:19:04.790 --> 00:19:09.140
about tyranny, meant
explicitly for the king,

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:11.720
should he ever hear tell of
it, which More rightly suspects

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:13.130
he will.

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:14.780
As the tower guard
character puts it,

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:17.780
marveling aloud in the
play, "In life and death,

00:19:17.780 --> 00:19:20.210
still merry, Sir Thomas More."

00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:24.440
In other words, the author
of Utopia knew how to laugh,

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:28.770
how to mock, how to jest, how
to speak doubly and ironically,

00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:31.760
even when discussing
serious life and death

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:35.100
matters of political justice.

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:37.650
I've got a little spider here.

00:19:37.650 --> 00:19:39.970
Hello, friend.

00:19:39.970 --> 00:19:42.830
It's a comic prop--

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:47.060
life and death questions
like justice and tyranny.

00:19:47.060 --> 00:19:50.150
He knew how to both
love and mock himself,

00:19:50.150 --> 00:19:52.820
his friends, and his king.

00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:56.390
By the way, if, as
I will argue here,

00:19:56.390 --> 00:19:59.330
More was a proto-Republican
that wanted to see England

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:04.640
move from a monarchy to
a full-blown republic.

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:06.530
You'd better learn how
to make fun of a king

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:09.020
without getting
your head cut off.

00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:11.750
How else do you do it short of
murdering your fellow citizens?

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:18.590
But you wouldn't know
More's Utopia holds up

00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:21.020
socialists for
ambivalent scrutiny,

00:20:21.020 --> 00:20:24.770
ironic mockery, and subtle
correction if you insist

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:28.790
on witlessly viewing the world
through the lens of unironic,

00:20:28.790 --> 00:20:31.100
literal-minded,
stripped down, unadorned

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:33.750
socialist grundlichkeit.

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:36.870
For example, if you
took a chilly turn

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:41.310
through the Alexandrovsky
Garden in the Kremlin in Moscow,

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:45.510
you will find a monument, an
ornate obelisk commemorating

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:49.620
the Romanov tsars of
yesteryear, with carved granite

00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:53.370
tens of feet tall and shining,
gold-leafed, double-eagle,

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:55.470
pyramidian cap.

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:58.140
The obelisk feels very
tsarist, very ornate,

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:01.470
like those potentates that
share both in sort of the East

00:21:01.470 --> 00:21:04.950
and the West that ruled
Russia for hundreds of years.

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:09.390
And yet, it was only
erected in 2013.

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:12.720
Or rather, it was
restored by Putin.

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:17.040
Lenin erased the original,
ordering Soviet riflemen

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:21.000
literally to shoot off the
original commemorative texts

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:24.330
in his 1918 campaign
of Soviet propaganda

00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:27.090
shortly after the
Bolshevik Revolution.

00:21:27.090 --> 00:21:30.660
Lenin replaced the
old tsarist monument

00:21:30.660 --> 00:21:34.530
or really just defaced it with
the brutalist and ponderously

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:35.195
named--

00:21:35.195 --> 00:21:36.570
(RUSSIAN ACCENT)
Monument Obelisk

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:39.510
To Outstanding Thinkers and
Personalities of the Struggle

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:42.360
for the Liberation of Workers.

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:45.990
No gold leaf on the Soviet
monument, no double eagle,

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:50.050
just a single,
heavy, cold stone.

00:21:50.050 --> 00:21:52.953
I just got bit by a spider.

00:21:52.953 --> 00:21:53.870
SPEAKER 1: [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:55.037
MATTHEW MEHAN: That's right.

00:21:55.037 --> 00:21:55.690
No.

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:57.892
It's a drone from AOC.

00:21:57.892 --> 00:21:59.756
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:02.090 --> 00:22:05.390
The Soviet obelisk
was simply a list--

00:22:05.390 --> 00:22:07.640
the Soviets liked
keeping lists--

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:10.340
commemorating revolutionary
thinkers and activists

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:14.000
that paved the way for the
Bolsheviks' moment of triumph

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:17.720
and the new socialist era
of glorious transition

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:20.480
to full communism for the
USSR, which stands for,

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:25.370
lest we forget, the Union of
Soviet Socialist Republics.

00:22:25.370 --> 00:22:27.980
In the precise middle of
the vertical list of names,

00:22:27.980 --> 00:22:31.160
below Karl Marx and our
very thorough friend,

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:34.550
Frederick Engels, and
amidst French anarchists

00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:37.190
and German socialist
party leaders,

00:22:37.190 --> 00:22:39.170
Lenin and his Soviet
propaganda saw

00:22:39.170 --> 00:22:42.470
fit to carve the
name of Thomas More,

00:22:42.470 --> 00:22:44.750
no doubt on account of
his famous and famously

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:48.110
misread book, Utopia, and
its fictitious island, which

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:50.960
has abolished private
property and is

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:55.700
said to have neither poverty,
nor envy, nor strife.

00:22:55.700 --> 00:22:59.630
Now this stark obelisk, like
so much of Soviet propaganda,

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:01.740
is a lie.

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:04.860
And like the rest of the
Soviet empire's house of lies,

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:07.650
the obelisk was
bulldozed unceremoniously

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:09.300
after communism's fall.

00:23:09.300 --> 00:23:12.620
But the lie of More's
Utopia, that it's socialist,

00:23:12.620 --> 00:23:14.490
still stands.

00:23:14.490 --> 00:23:18.570
If one thoughtlessly skims
Utopia or willfully cherry

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:21.630
picks from its lengthy
and ironic dialogue,

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:23.640
it's possible to think
the author advocates

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:27.030
socialism and the abolition
of private property.

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:29.400
After all, Raphael Hythloday,
the dubious traveler

00:23:29.400 --> 00:23:32.850
in the dialogue, who More
compares to the famous liar

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:37.643
Odysseus, does offer the
following lengthy speech.

00:23:37.643 --> 00:23:39.060
This is Raphael
Hythloday speaking

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:41.010
at length, grundlichkeit.

00:23:41.010 --> 00:23:43.680
"The most prudent man-- that
is Plato in his "Republic--"

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:46.650
easily foresaw that
there could be only one

00:23:46.650 --> 00:23:48.900
way to public
well-being, by decreeing

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:51.870
the equality of possessions,
which I do not think

00:23:51.870 --> 00:23:53.550
could ever be observed
when individuals

00:23:53.550 --> 00:23:55.130
have private ownership.

00:23:55.130 --> 00:23:59.020
For when each person, by a
definite right and title"--

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:03.420
remember that-- "sweeps up
as much as can be to himself,

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:06.150
the entire supply of
goods, no matter how large,

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:09.540
is divided up among
only a few persons,

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:11.940
leaving scarcity
for everyone else.

00:24:11.940 --> 00:24:14.430
It generally happens then that
each group really deserves

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:17.370
the destiny of the other group,
since the few are rapacious,

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:20.170
shameless, and useless,
while the many, by contrast,

00:24:20.170 --> 00:24:22.590
are modest and simple, and
by their daily industry,

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:24.420
more kindly disposed
to the public interest

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:26.017
than they are to themselves.

00:24:26.017 --> 00:24:28.350
And this is why I'm fully
persuaded that goods cannot be

00:24:28.350 --> 00:24:31.980
distributed in an equitable or
just way or that anything can

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:35.250
turn out well in human affairs
unless ownership is completely

00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:36.200
abolished.

00:24:36.200 --> 00:24:38.850
And on the other hand,
as long as it remains,

00:24:38.850 --> 00:24:42.810
so also will remain an anxiety
producing and inescapable load

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:45.360
of hardships and poverty
for the largest part,

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:49.190
and the best part, by
far, of the human race."

00:24:49.190 --> 00:24:50.460
OK.

00:24:50.460 --> 00:24:54.540
Long quote, straight
up socialism.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:57.510
But to think that this
is More's position

00:24:57.510 --> 00:25:00.360
or that it is the final verdict
of the dialogue of Utopia

00:25:00.360 --> 00:25:04.320
would be like witlessly cherry
picking Macbeth's Act V despair

00:25:04.320 --> 00:25:07.230
speech, concluding
that Shakespeare

00:25:07.230 --> 00:25:09.330
and the entire
tragedy of Macbeth

00:25:09.330 --> 00:25:12.120
agree with the character
Macbeth when he says that "Life

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:16.000
is a tale told by an idiot,
full of sound and fury,

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:18.600
signifying nothing."

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:21.930
Perhaps Macbeth had read
too many socialist pamphlets

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:23.520
that day.

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:25.080
Would the socialist
interpretation

00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:31.170
of More's subtle and dialogic
text work less careless

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:33.630
and more, dare I
say it, thorough, we

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:35.490
wouldn't be in this position.

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:38.890
It is perhaps more than
simply an amusing irony

00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:41.730
then that the character
of Raphael Hythloday

00:25:41.730 --> 00:25:44.070
loves overwhelming
his interlocutors

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:47.070
with very thorough,
very lengthy monologues,

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:50.610
not unlike Friedrich Engels and
his fellow socialist, Dr. E.

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:51.750
Duhring.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:54.630
Those who think so simply
and simplistically,

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:57.090
as to suggest
seriously and literally

00:25:57.090 --> 00:26:00.150
that we ought to remove all
private property to solve

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:02.490
our political ills,
are the same sorts

00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:05.790
that can't, for lack of
wit, follow a dialogue

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:08.790
and can't engage in one either.

00:26:08.790 --> 00:26:11.460
Like Syndrome from Brad
Bird's Incredibles,

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:14.460
they're too busy monologuing.

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:18.210
Literary wit, listening,
conversing, discerning,

00:26:18.210 --> 00:26:21.660
seeing double meaning,
irony, humor, paradox,

00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:25.470
the habit of looking at life, at
human behavior, or at the city

00:26:25.470 --> 00:26:28.740
and realizing that what's
before your eyes is a tale

00:26:28.740 --> 00:26:31.410
told by no idiot,
which signifies

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:33.750
much more than nothing.

00:26:33.750 --> 00:26:36.150
But that's the position
of atheist materialism

00:26:36.150 --> 00:26:39.780
that undergirds socialism,
is there's nothing but meat

00:26:39.780 --> 00:26:41.160
in front of you.

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:43.170
You don't have to
have a double vision.

00:26:43.170 --> 00:26:45.570
You don't see the
Providence of God.

00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:48.150
You don't see an immortal soul.

00:26:48.150 --> 00:26:52.500
You don't see mind,
principle, spirit.

00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:57.550
You just see meat on a rock,
hurtling through the dark.

00:26:57.550 --> 00:27:02.635
It's a very monologic way
of thinking about the world.

00:27:02.635 --> 00:27:03.750
OK.

00:27:03.750 --> 00:27:05.151
That was pretty ponderous.

00:27:05.151 --> 00:27:06.474
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:10.470
So these ponderous and
thorough monologuers,

00:27:10.470 --> 00:27:13.830
masters of grundlichkeit,
tend to lack the subtlety

00:27:13.830 --> 00:27:19.380
and receptivity, that is,
the wit required for wisdom,

00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:22.980
for good judgment about
what people say, about texts

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:26.700
that people write, and about
the cities and societies

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:29.070
that we need to build.

00:27:29.070 --> 00:27:31.170
That is, the socialist
character type,

00:27:31.170 --> 00:27:34.290
which More lampoons in the
person of Raphael Hythloday

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:39.270
in Utopia, tends to have
grundlichkeit when expounding

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:43.080
their own theories of Utopian
and scientific socialism,

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:46.410
but also tend to lack that
same thoroughness when it comes

00:27:46.410 --> 00:27:48.920
to being attentive
to reality itself

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:51.270
and what other people
are telling them.

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:55.930
And that reality is that
More's character in Utopia

00:27:55.930 --> 00:27:58.690
strongly objects to
Raphael Hythloday's

00:27:58.690 --> 00:28:01.210
socialist institutions,
both in book one

00:28:01.210 --> 00:28:03.100
and in book two of Utopia.

00:28:03.100 --> 00:28:05.890
Here's More's
objection from book one

00:28:05.890 --> 00:28:08.680
that he had to wait to give
because Raphael Hythloday went

00:28:08.680 --> 00:28:10.900
on and on for a long time
before he could finally

00:28:10.900 --> 00:28:14.720
get a word in edgewise
in the dialogue.

00:28:14.720 --> 00:28:17.470
"But on the contrary,"
More said, "it seems to me

00:28:17.470 --> 00:28:20.140
that life can never be lived
with any convenience, where

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:22.240
all things are owned in common.

00:28:22.240 --> 00:28:24.550
For how could there
be a good supply

00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:27.670
of things with everybody
trying to get out of work,

00:28:27.670 --> 00:28:29.770
not being urged by
the profit motive

00:28:29.770 --> 00:28:33.470
and becoming lazy by relying
on the work of others?

00:28:33.470 --> 00:28:36.430
But even if they are spurred
to work by the scarcity, when

00:28:36.430 --> 00:28:39.670
nobody is able lawfully to
protect as his own what has he

00:28:39.670 --> 00:28:42.580
has acquired, is
it not inevitable

00:28:42.580 --> 00:28:46.003
that everyone will suffer from
perpetual murder and sedition,

00:28:46.003 --> 00:28:48.420
especially when the authority
and reverence of magistrates

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:49.360
is gone?

00:28:49.360 --> 00:28:52.570
For how there could be any place
for that when the people cannot

00:28:52.570 --> 00:28:54.940
be distinguished among
themselves in any way,

00:28:54.940 --> 00:28:57.580
I cannot imagine."

00:28:57.580 --> 00:29:00.160
And yet, despite this
very clear counterargument

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:02.560
and others from Thomas
More, the character

00:29:02.560 --> 00:29:06.940
in his own dialogue, the bogus
claim that More's Utopia is

00:29:06.940 --> 00:29:09.430
really a work of socialist
and communist theory

00:29:09.430 --> 00:29:12.160
is still proudly trumpeted,
both from on high

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:13.630
in the ivory tower--

00:29:13.630 --> 00:29:15.550
for instance, Cambridge
University's Terry

00:29:15.550 --> 00:29:19.360
Eagleton, and from on low in
the digital swamps of Wikipedia

00:29:19.360 --> 00:29:23.310
on the-- surprise, surprise--
entry for utopian socialism.

00:29:23.310 --> 00:29:26.800
Now to be fair, it may be
that Utopia is considered

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:30.250
a socialist book because
at least some socialists,

00:29:30.250 --> 00:29:32.770
trained as they are
for straightforward,

00:29:32.770 --> 00:29:37.030
scientific accounts of political
economy and radical revolution,

00:29:37.030 --> 00:29:40.330
cannot read a text for
metaphor and irony.

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:43.085
Being able to read for
subtlety takes a good deal

00:29:43.085 --> 00:29:44.710
of liberal arts
training, like the kind

00:29:44.710 --> 00:29:47.020
we offer here at Hillsdale.

00:29:47.020 --> 00:29:50.920
Perhaps it's as simple as to
say they are just too literal

00:29:50.920 --> 00:29:54.662
minded, lacking a literary
education in true wit.

00:29:54.662 --> 00:29:56.620
Or maybe they're just
mentally drained from all

00:29:56.620 --> 00:29:57.490
the pamphleteering.

00:29:57.490 --> 00:29:59.960
[LAUGHTER]

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:01.112
Maybe.

00:30:01.112 --> 00:30:03.590
I tend to think that it gets
them excited for some reason.

00:30:07.070 --> 00:30:13.160
Being able to read for subtlety,
that skill not being there--

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:18.380
maybe it's just that
they have subtlety,

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:22.250
but they actually refuse
to read for subtlety

00:30:22.250 --> 00:30:25.610
in a way that takes Utopia's
ironic and mocking presentation

00:30:25.610 --> 00:30:28.580
of their own position
seriously because they're

00:30:28.580 --> 00:30:31.280
more interested in the
political project of socialism

00:30:31.280 --> 00:30:34.210
than being truthful about
what other people have said.

00:30:34.210 --> 00:30:37.190
That is, they don't
mind lying to you.

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:40.850
That was Solzhenitsyn's big
point in The Gulag Archipelago.

00:30:40.850 --> 00:30:42.952
Remember, Professor
John Grant, the head

00:30:42.952 --> 00:30:44.660
of the politics
department here, came out

00:30:44.660 --> 00:30:47.540
to Kirby a few years back.

00:30:47.540 --> 00:30:51.343
The lie-- they want
to hold the lie.

00:30:51.343 --> 00:30:56.310
And Solzhenitsyn was like,
you got to speak the truth.

00:30:56.310 --> 00:30:57.960
Or perhaps they
simply can't bear

00:30:57.960 --> 00:31:00.820
to be mocked, so they just
block out the subtle reasoning.

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:02.670
Maybe it's subconscious.

00:31:02.670 --> 00:31:04.830
Another possible obstacle
to reading Utopia

00:31:04.830 --> 00:31:09.720
wittily is that most socialists
think too little about politics

00:31:09.720 --> 00:31:12.270
and think much more narrowly
about economic questions

00:31:12.270 --> 00:31:14.310
concerning the
means of production.

00:31:14.310 --> 00:31:17.700
They tend to deride political
and constitutional fixes

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:19.890
as merely bourgeois
institutions,

00:31:19.890 --> 00:31:21.510
half measures, to be pitied.

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:25.330
They must be swept away by the
revolution as soon as possible.

00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:28.230
That means they do
not consider rightly

00:31:28.230 --> 00:31:30.840
nor respect the fact
that Thomas More is,

00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:34.290
in fact, a proto-Republican.

00:31:34.290 --> 00:31:35.670
He's a proto-Republican thinker.

00:31:35.670 --> 00:31:39.420
That is, More's ironic
criticism of private property,

00:31:39.420 --> 00:31:41.360
in the voice of
Raphael Hythloday,

00:31:41.360 --> 00:31:44.250
is not the radical
attacks of socialists

00:31:44.250 --> 00:31:46.650
on a widely distributed
and legally held

00:31:46.650 --> 00:31:50.790
democratic system of private
property, generosity,

00:31:50.790 --> 00:31:54.630
and equality, like we have or
strive to have here in the US.

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:57.150
Rather, More's teachings
on private property

00:31:57.150 --> 00:32:01.140
are attempting to moderate the
radically oligarchic position

00:32:01.140 --> 00:32:04.320
of the landed
aristocracy of England.

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:06.060
I'm reminded of
one of my teachers,

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:09.780
another Hillsdale professor, Dr.
Tom West, when he would lament

00:32:09.780 --> 00:32:12.330
the lockup of federal
lands by the US

00:32:12.330 --> 00:32:15.690
Senate and the Department
of Interior that

00:32:15.690 --> 00:32:18.060
couldn't be accessed for
private property ownership

00:32:18.060 --> 00:32:20.070
by the citizenry.

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:21.810
Instead, imagine this.

00:32:21.810 --> 00:32:24.240
More was looking at an
unelected Senate, namely

00:32:24.240 --> 00:32:27.120
the House of Lords, the
members of which personally

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:30.150
own the land and have permanent
claim to it in perpetuity

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:33.630
for their descendants
forever and ever, amen.

00:32:33.630 --> 00:32:35.610
It's not surprising
that radical socialists

00:32:35.610 --> 00:32:39.000
can't see More's attempt to
moderate the claims of property

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:42.600
ownership so that property
could be treated in a way more

00:32:42.600 --> 00:32:45.210
in line with common
sense and natural reason,

00:32:45.210 --> 00:32:48.760
changing the law to something
closer to what we have now.

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:51.660
But socialists probably
do see, at least,

00:32:51.660 --> 00:32:55.260
that More is honoring
admirable qualities of at least

00:32:55.260 --> 00:32:58.950
some socialists or
some accounts of what

00:32:58.950 --> 00:33:02.220
we might call soft
socialism, for instance,

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:04.530
detachment from material goods.

00:33:04.530 --> 00:33:05.588
We should be detached.

00:33:05.588 --> 00:33:06.630
We can't take it with us.

00:33:06.630 --> 00:33:08.213
You don't want your
things to own you.

00:33:08.213 --> 00:33:09.510
You want to own your things.

00:33:09.510 --> 00:33:11.520
That's very admirable.

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:15.660
Love of the poor, that's
very good, see Jesus.

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:18.750
And yes, ridicule for
the folly-filled idea

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:23.950
that you and your family have
a given plot of land by nature.

00:33:23.950 --> 00:33:25.830
In other words,
the ideology behind

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:29.430
landed aristocracy, which
we Americans expressly

00:33:29.430 --> 00:33:31.290
forbid in our Constitution,
in part because

00:33:31.290 --> 00:33:36.210
of the witty, proto-Republican,
counsels of writers like More.

00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:39.750
But socialists don't see these
subtle dynamics in Utopia

00:33:39.750 --> 00:33:45.000
because those ironies
detail and serve

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:48.030
the purpose of weakening
the claims of nobility

00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:51.510
and encouraging the civic
spiritedness of generosity

00:33:51.510 --> 00:33:52.860
and friendship.

00:33:52.860 --> 00:33:55.860
And a proper, witty, and
accurate reading of Utopia

00:33:55.860 --> 00:33:59.130
does not support the socialists'
historical narratives

00:33:59.130 --> 00:34:01.080
nor their radical
policy to abolish

00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:04.270
all or most private property for
the sake of socialist control

00:34:04.270 --> 00:34:05.940
of the means of production.

00:34:05.940 --> 00:34:09.719
Socialists seem not to suspect
More's strong, yet subtle

00:34:09.719 --> 00:34:14.489
mockery of their very dangerous
intellectual vices, their love

00:34:14.489 --> 00:34:17.159
of monologues, of
having their way,

00:34:17.159 --> 00:34:19.260
of living like kings
in their opinions,

00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:24.120
unhampered by the messy details
of actual political reality.

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:28.170
It's true that More respects
the socialist character type.

00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:32.190
Raphael Hythloday's first
name does mean healer of God.

00:34:32.190 --> 00:34:34.409
Just so, the socialist
character type,

00:34:34.409 --> 00:34:37.929
as an admirably ardent
desire for perfection--

00:34:37.929 --> 00:34:41.429
that's commendable-- is
worth praising for a society

00:34:41.429 --> 00:34:43.440
where no one suffers.

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:45.360
Never going to happen,
but, obviously, it's

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:47.650
something that theoretically
you would like to see.

00:34:47.650 --> 00:34:49.860
We're all hoping for
heaven, for something

00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:54.090
like that, where every
tear shall be wiped away,

00:34:54.090 --> 00:34:55.145
and where Engels--

00:34:55.145 --> 00:34:57.360
or as Engels put it,
where everyone holds,

00:34:57.360 --> 00:34:59.280
quote, "The common
property of all

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:01.800
to be worked for the
common good of all."

00:35:01.800 --> 00:35:05.210
Or as Matt Bell was
quoting earlier, heaven,

00:35:05.210 --> 00:35:07.560
but you don't even need it.

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:09.420
It all sounds nice.

00:35:09.420 --> 00:35:11.310
It sounds lovely.

00:35:11.310 --> 00:35:14.070
Socialism does sound good.

00:35:14.070 --> 00:35:16.350
Witty Sir Thomas More
was aware of just how

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:18.780
lovely socialism sounded.

00:35:18.780 --> 00:35:21.510
But just as it takes a certain
sharp-sighted wit to see when

00:35:21.510 --> 00:35:24.450
an offer is too good
to be true, likewise--

00:35:24.450 --> 00:35:27.000
I just saw a billboard, driving
from the Detroit Airport

00:35:27.000 --> 00:35:27.600
today--

00:35:27.600 --> 00:35:29.068
free college.

00:35:29.068 --> 00:35:31.110
That was all the billboard
said and some address.

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:34.500
You're like, probably not.

00:35:34.500 --> 00:35:36.610
Something's up.

00:35:36.610 --> 00:35:39.480
Hold on to your wallet.

00:35:39.480 --> 00:35:47.620
Likewise, More seeks in
Utopia to train our own wit,

00:35:47.620 --> 00:35:50.260
to see the difference between
a political order that

00:35:50.260 --> 00:35:52.450
sounds good and
one that actually

00:35:52.450 --> 00:35:55.810
looks good in reality,
one that is good and not

00:35:55.810 --> 00:35:57.820
just good in theory.

00:35:57.820 --> 00:36:01.150
That's why the proto-socialist
character of Raphael Hythloday

00:36:01.150 --> 00:36:05.140
isn't just God's healer nor
simply a skilled speaker

00:36:05.140 --> 00:36:06.460
in nonsense.

00:36:06.460 --> 00:36:10.090
He has both names together
because nothing that is

00:36:10.090 --> 00:36:15.520
is all bad nor, in this world
at least, all good in all

00:36:15.520 --> 00:36:17.750
respects at all times.

00:36:17.750 --> 00:36:21.180
This sort of double
vision, this ability

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:24.300
to see something admirable
in socialist impulses

00:36:24.300 --> 00:36:27.660
while also realizing the
dangerous and tyrannical,

00:36:27.660 --> 00:36:31.200
deeply tyrannical impulses
that lurk beneath them,

00:36:31.200 --> 00:36:34.620
this sort of witty, wise habit
of looking at life and politics

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:38.280
to see what it is, what
you wish it could be,

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:40.410
and the difference between
prudential political

00:36:40.410 --> 00:36:43.800
improvements and radically
dangerous, dangerously radical,

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:47.400
thoroughly stupid, and stupidly
thorough political change

00:36:47.400 --> 00:36:50.730
is revealed in Utopia as the
difference between dialogue

00:36:50.730 --> 00:36:54.245
and monologue, between
More and Hythloday,

00:36:54.245 --> 00:36:58.800
and between what we might call
Republican wit and tyrannical

00:36:58.800 --> 00:37:02.780
and socialist grundlichkeit.

00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:06.530
And More's Utopia, centuries
before the full flowering

00:37:06.530 --> 00:37:08.780
of socialism and
communism, offers

00:37:08.780 --> 00:37:11.720
a deep meditation on
prudential politics

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:15.140
and a powerful and witty
critique of such thoroughgoing

00:37:15.140 --> 00:37:17.630
tyrannies, the sort
that is enslaved

00:37:17.630 --> 00:37:20.840
to a witless idea of
perfection into the sound

00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:23.870
of their own monologic voices.

00:37:23.870 --> 00:37:26.930
At the close of Utopia,
after a long tale

00:37:26.930 --> 00:37:29.930
from Hythloday full of ironic
and conflicting statements

00:37:29.930 --> 00:37:31.370
designed to help
the reader think

00:37:31.370 --> 00:37:35.210
about politics and justice,
More's own character

00:37:35.210 --> 00:37:37.850
thinks that the laws and
customs of the Utopians

00:37:37.850 --> 00:37:39.770
seem very absurdly established.

00:37:39.770 --> 00:37:42.985
But, he says, "Most of
all in that feature,

00:37:42.985 --> 00:37:44.360
which is the
principle foundation

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:47.840
of their whole structure,
I mean their common life

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:52.910
and subsistence without
any exchange of money."

00:37:52.910 --> 00:37:55.220
Utopia's lack of
customs and laws

00:37:55.220 --> 00:37:57.560
protecting private
property and commerce

00:37:57.560 --> 00:38:03.380
More emphasizes, quote, "utterly
overthrows all the nobility,"

00:38:03.380 --> 00:38:07.100
taking from the
nobles that word.

00:38:07.100 --> 00:38:09.590
If you take away private
property of all the people

00:38:09.590 --> 00:38:12.470
of the commonwealth, the
commons, "you overthrow--

00:38:12.470 --> 00:38:15.860
utterly overthrow all the
nobility, magnificence--"

00:38:15.860 --> 00:38:17.702
that's a kingly term--

00:38:17.702 --> 00:38:21.200
"splendor--" another
kingly-- "and majesty--"

00:38:21.200 --> 00:38:22.640
another kingly term--

00:38:22.640 --> 00:38:25.460
"which are, in the estimation
of the common people,

00:38:25.460 --> 00:38:30.680
the true glories and ornaments
of the commonwealth."

00:38:30.680 --> 00:38:33.000
The Latin here is more telling.

00:38:33.000 --> 00:38:34.400
And forgive me--

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:36.065
[SPEAKING LATIN]

00:38:41.150 --> 00:38:45.370
Thomas More is
carefully, wittily,

00:38:45.370 --> 00:38:49.210
and subtly a champion of
democratic republicanism

00:38:49.210 --> 00:38:53.680
in an age of monarchs
and monologues, a writer

00:38:53.680 --> 00:38:56.650
responsible for a masterwork
of Western literature

00:38:56.650 --> 00:38:58.810
full of Socratic irony
designed to educate

00:38:58.810 --> 00:39:02.320
a free people for personal
virtue and self-government

00:39:02.320 --> 00:39:04.840
by means of a city and
speech like that of Plato's

00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:07.960
in the "Republic" or more
like Cicero's in the "De re

00:39:07.960 --> 00:39:09.670
publica."

00:39:09.670 --> 00:39:11.590
It was this man,
Thomas More, whom

00:39:11.590 --> 00:39:15.550
Lenin, in one of history's
monumental ironies,

00:39:15.550 --> 00:39:19.390
deigned to call comrade.

00:39:19.390 --> 00:39:22.750
I imagine that the wily
and witty Sir Thomas

00:39:22.750 --> 00:39:24.910
More, the statesman
who Shakespeare tells

00:39:24.910 --> 00:39:28.480
us loved to be merry, would
have received this honor

00:39:28.480 --> 00:39:32.920
if he were alive today with
a wry smile and his trademark

00:39:32.920 --> 00:39:33.875
irony.

00:39:33.875 --> 00:39:35.500
He might have said
something like this.

00:39:35.500 --> 00:39:37.300
Thank you, comrades.

00:39:37.300 --> 00:39:40.030
I had thought that the Union
of Soviet Socialist Republics

00:39:40.030 --> 00:39:42.190
would have better left
me out in the cold,

00:39:42.190 --> 00:39:45.910
but you have honored me in a
way no good man should ever

00:39:45.910 --> 00:39:46.859
hope for.

00:39:46.859 --> 00:39:48.855
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:51.822 --> 00:39:53.530
You can make those
kinds of jokes all day

00:39:53.530 --> 00:39:56.890
if you read Utopia as
many times as I have.

00:39:56.890 --> 00:40:01.840
That's what it's designed
to do, fashion your wit,

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:03.860
shape your wit.

00:40:03.860 --> 00:40:07.160
Now I'll stop monologuing and
start dialoguing with you.

00:40:07.160 --> 00:40:07.850
Thank you.

00:40:07.850 --> 00:40:10.790
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:17.161 --> 00:40:18.820
SPEAKER 2: Thank you Dr. Mehan.

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:22.152
We now have time for Q&amp;A. Please
make your way to the microphone

00:40:22.152 --> 00:40:23.110
if you have a question.

00:40:25.906 --> 00:40:27.448
AUDIENCE: I just
have short question.

00:40:27.448 --> 00:40:31.786
I can't get to the microphone,
but I understand that Utopia

00:40:31.786 --> 00:40:32.974
was written in Latin?

00:40:32.974 --> 00:40:34.141
MATTHEW MEHAN: That's right.

00:40:34.141 --> 00:40:36.600
AUDIENCE: And it is able
to be read in English?

00:40:36.600 --> 00:40:37.600
MATTHEW MEHAN: Mhm.

00:40:37.600 --> 00:40:38.100
Yeah.

00:40:38.100 --> 00:40:40.770
There's a number
of translations.

00:40:40.770 --> 00:40:42.270
There's ones free online.

00:40:42.270 --> 00:40:45.060
There's a really excellent
translation that's actually

00:40:45.060 --> 00:40:50.670
coming out from The Essential
Works of Thomas More

00:40:50.670 --> 00:40:54.720
this January, which was put
together over the course of 20

00:40:54.720 --> 00:40:59.820
years with a professor, Jerry
Wegemer, who I studied with,

00:40:59.820 --> 00:41:05.880
but also his co-editor
is Dr. Stephen Smith

00:41:05.880 --> 00:41:08.010
here at Hillsdale.

00:41:08.010 --> 00:41:10.890
So it's actually--
that book's coming out

00:41:10.890 --> 00:41:13.350
in January with the
best translation,

00:41:13.350 --> 00:41:16.020
the best notes
that really explain

00:41:16.020 --> 00:41:20.130
what is going on in this text
and all of More's major texts.

00:41:20.130 --> 00:41:21.390
That's coming in January.

00:41:21.390 --> 00:41:25.380
And for a mind of
this caliber who's

00:41:25.380 --> 00:41:29.460
written this many classics and
has had this much influence,

00:41:29.460 --> 00:41:31.030
this happens once
every 100 years.

00:41:31.030 --> 00:41:34.217
So that's something to pay
attention to in January.

00:41:34.217 --> 00:41:35.550
But that's the best translation.

00:41:35.550 --> 00:41:36.720
I've seen it.

00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:39.420
I've heckled over some of
its footnotes with Steve

00:41:39.420 --> 00:41:40.170
a couple of times.

00:41:40.170 --> 00:41:44.770
But that's coming
out in January.

00:41:44.770 --> 00:41:46.047
Yes, please.

00:41:46.047 --> 00:41:47.630
AUDIENCE: I think
of Marx and Engels--

00:41:47.630 --> 00:41:49.463
AUDIENCE: Can you go
to the microphone, sir?

00:41:49.463 --> 00:41:51.505
SPEAKER 2: Please make
your way to the microphone

00:41:51.505 --> 00:41:52.480
if you have a question.

00:41:56.260 --> 00:41:58.267
MATTHEW MEHAN: Oh, sorry.

00:41:58.267 --> 00:42:02.100
AUDIENCE: Not sure
if this is working.

00:42:02.100 --> 00:42:03.353
MATTHEW MEHAN: The long walk.

00:42:03.353 --> 00:42:05.020
We'll take one while
he's getting there.

00:42:05.020 --> 00:42:05.520
Please.

00:42:08.120 --> 00:42:10.700
AUDIENCE: Thank you
for your talk today.

00:42:10.700 --> 00:42:13.510
I remember-- I'm getting
to the point in my life

00:42:13.510 --> 00:42:16.020
where I'm thinking, long ago,
when the dinosaurs were young,

00:42:16.020 --> 00:42:17.930
I used to tell my daughter.

00:42:17.930 --> 00:42:21.470
I remember-- and this is more
of a current cultural thing,

00:42:21.470 --> 00:42:25.400
but I think it's tangentially
related to Thomas More,

00:42:25.400 --> 00:42:31.820
that we had a richness
of comedy in our culture,

00:42:31.820 --> 00:42:37.680
from broad slapstick to
incisive, intellectual wit.

00:42:37.680 --> 00:42:40.610
And it seems like
today, there's no humor

00:42:40.610 --> 00:42:42.650
because the
socialists have taken

00:42:42.650 --> 00:42:45.320
over the mechanisms of comedy.

00:42:45.320 --> 00:42:47.210
And through this
PC nonsense, which

00:42:47.210 --> 00:42:51.060
is nothing more than an
intellectual and a humor

00:42:51.060 --> 00:42:54.858
straitjacket, is--

00:42:54.858 --> 00:42:55.650
comedy's not funny.

00:42:55.650 --> 00:42:58.520
It's either
scatological, or it's

00:42:58.520 --> 00:43:02.660
just dreadfully morbid,
and dull, and malicious.

00:43:02.660 --> 00:43:06.632
I just wanted to comment
from Thomas More to this.

00:43:06.632 --> 00:43:07.556
Thank you.

00:43:11.468 --> 00:43:12.260
MATTHEW MEHAN: Yes.

00:43:12.260 --> 00:43:14.148
[LAUGHTER]

00:43:18.880 --> 00:43:22.060
There are comics,
I think, oftentimes

00:43:22.060 --> 00:43:24.490
some really excellent
wit is wasted

00:43:24.490 --> 00:43:27.400
on scatological material,
some brilliant people who

00:43:27.400 --> 00:43:33.160
decide to go hide in some filth
that is sort of not really

00:43:33.160 --> 00:43:35.650
fit for dignified enjoyment.

00:43:35.650 --> 00:43:36.680
So there's that problem.

00:43:36.680 --> 00:43:38.320
I think there is
still wit out there.

00:43:38.320 --> 00:43:42.080
It's oftentimes
submerged in some filth.

00:43:42.080 --> 00:43:47.790
But you do see some rebellion.

00:43:47.790 --> 00:43:50.420
And the comics and the comedians
are sort of fighting back,

00:43:50.420 --> 00:43:53.800
even the director of
the latest Joker movie.

00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:57.380
In protest, Joker is actually
a silent, grundlichkeit-type

00:43:57.380 --> 00:43:57.880
film.

00:43:57.880 --> 00:44:01.210
It's this ponderous,
dark, brooding thing.

00:44:01.210 --> 00:44:03.650
He's a comic director.

00:44:03.650 --> 00:44:05.740
But he said, I can't
do comedy for now.

00:44:05.740 --> 00:44:07.240
So you're just going
to have to deal

00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:09.670
with that, almost like a
protest, a sort of laughter

00:44:09.670 --> 00:44:11.280
strike.

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:13.840
So I don't know if the
clowns are going to save us,

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:19.580
but there is sort of
some tomfoolery that's

00:44:19.580 --> 00:44:20.600
gaining steam, I think.

00:44:23.097 --> 00:44:23.680
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:44:23.680 --> 00:44:28.050
If I think of the inspiration
for Marx and Engels,

00:44:28.050 --> 00:44:30.720
communism coming from
the sweatshops of England

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:33.480
in the 1820s, '30s,
'40s, and '50s,

00:44:33.480 --> 00:44:36.030
where does Thomas More
get his inspiration

00:44:36.030 --> 00:44:39.846
from for writing about
socialism the way he did?

00:44:39.846 --> 00:44:42.180
MATTHEW MEHAN: Good question.

00:44:42.180 --> 00:44:45.600
So the enclosure movement is
actually featured and discussed

00:44:45.600 --> 00:44:48.810
in book one of the dialogue.

00:44:48.810 --> 00:44:55.110
And for him, he's very
upset with the nobility

00:44:55.110 --> 00:44:59.760
making property right
claims, evicting

00:44:59.760 --> 00:45:01.830
peasants who've lived
in the same family

00:45:01.830 --> 00:45:03.700
home for 1,000 years.

00:45:03.700 --> 00:45:05.460
But because it wasn't
on a piece of paper

00:45:05.460 --> 00:45:09.170
and it wasn't enforced
by the House of Lords,

00:45:09.170 --> 00:45:12.630
the common people's common
wealth, their property,

00:45:12.630 --> 00:45:15.700
their homes, were
judged worthless.

00:45:15.700 --> 00:45:20.730
So that gets him thinking
about radical claims

00:45:20.730 --> 00:45:23.887
to property ownership
of the lords.

00:45:23.887 --> 00:45:25.470
So he's taking aim
at the lords who've

00:45:25.470 --> 00:45:30.270
kicked all these people off
in order to make sheep folds.

00:45:30.270 --> 00:45:32.950
But what's happened is
they've lost their homes,

00:45:32.950 --> 00:45:34.800
so they can't farm.

00:45:34.800 --> 00:45:38.070
And they wind up taking to
the streets, turning to crime.

00:45:38.070 --> 00:45:40.710
And hangings spike
thousands of percent.

00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:42.030
It's just a bloodbath.

00:45:42.030 --> 00:45:43.820
Every day, they're
hanging people.

00:45:43.820 --> 00:45:46.060
So More is responding to that.

00:45:46.060 --> 00:45:48.510
But if you don't
pay attention, you

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:51.980
go, oh, look, he's mad
at private property.

00:45:51.980 --> 00:45:52.992
No.

00:45:52.992 --> 00:45:54.075
He loves private property.

00:45:54.075 --> 00:45:57.270
He thinks it's been
violated by the nobility.

00:45:57.270 --> 00:45:59.970
A specious private
property claim of the elite

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:04.320
has robbed all of the peasants
of their commonly held homes

00:46:04.320 --> 00:46:10.500
for 1,000 years.

00:46:10.500 --> 00:46:15.980
AUDIENCE: So I've not
read the Utopia yet

00:46:15.980 --> 00:46:19.220
or Plato's "Republic."

00:46:19.220 --> 00:46:22.040
I've just begun reading
The Gulag Archipelago,

00:46:22.040 --> 00:46:26.406
but I have read quite a
majority of Plato's "Timaeus

00:46:26.406 --> 00:46:33.500
and Critias," which tells the
story of Atlantis, which is--

00:46:33.500 --> 00:46:36.920
the usual story we've
all heard some version of

00:46:36.920 --> 00:46:42.740
is that it was a
utopia-type paradise that

00:46:42.740 --> 00:46:47.570
fell because it turned to
decadence and just filth,

00:46:47.570 --> 00:46:54.770
and was, of course, destroyed
in a tsunami or a flood.

00:46:54.770 --> 00:46:56.340
That's the very
end of the story.

00:46:56.340 --> 00:47:02.750
But also, the moral, I guess,
and social aspect or lesson

00:47:02.750 --> 00:47:09.710
of the story was that a
utopia in the paradise sense,

00:47:09.710 --> 00:47:15.090
at least if you're talking
about a government or socialism,

00:47:15.090 --> 00:47:17.700
either one of any
sort, is that it's

00:47:17.700 --> 00:47:22.920
ultimately unattainable
and uncreatable because it

00:47:22.920 --> 00:47:27.990
falls apart and is badly
managed so much that there's

00:47:27.990 --> 00:47:31.035
nothing you can do with it.

00:47:31.035 --> 00:47:32.290
SPEAKER 2: [INAUDIBLE]

00:47:32.290 --> 00:47:37.460
AUDIENCE: So the question
is, so in this way,

00:47:37.460 --> 00:47:43.865
are the two stories of
Atlantis and Utopia, is that--

00:47:43.865 --> 00:47:46.110
am I accurate about
the similarity?

00:47:46.110 --> 00:47:50.610
And what seems to be
the goal of socialism?

00:47:50.610 --> 00:47:54.580
When you ask the socialists,
they have no vision.

00:47:54.580 --> 00:47:57.180
So what is their coherent plan?

00:47:57.180 --> 00:48:01.870
And is that a
correct similarity?

00:48:01.870 --> 00:48:03.850
MATTHEW MEHAN: That was
a lot of grundlichkeit

00:48:03.850 --> 00:48:05.391
for an awesome question.

00:48:05.391 --> 00:48:07.355
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:10.880 --> 00:48:14.042
You're absolutely right to see--

00:48:14.042 --> 00:48:15.993
Critias, he says,
I've got this place

00:48:15.993 --> 00:48:18.160
I'm going to tell you about
that seems very strange,

00:48:18.160 --> 00:48:19.348
but every word of it's true.

00:48:19.348 --> 00:48:20.890
So it's very much
Raphael Hythloday--

00:48:20.890 --> 00:48:22.720
I swear, no place exists.

00:48:22.720 --> 00:48:24.490
Utopia exists.

00:48:24.490 --> 00:48:25.750
Sure, buddy.

00:48:25.750 --> 00:48:29.230
He's a traveler,
kind of sketchy.

00:48:29.230 --> 00:48:33.805
And he also says it was his
90-year-old grandfather who

00:48:33.805 --> 00:48:35.180
told it to him
when he was eight.

00:48:35.180 --> 00:48:36.428
So you're like, what, really?

00:48:36.428 --> 00:48:38.470
You're going to have a
detailed political program

00:48:38.470 --> 00:48:40.600
from that exchange?

00:48:40.600 --> 00:48:44.500
So it's that same dubious I've
got a perfect ideal that's

00:48:44.500 --> 00:48:47.240
very questionable.

00:48:47.240 --> 00:48:49.210
So yeah, I think
More's definitely--

00:48:49.210 --> 00:48:50.860
he translated
Plato's "Republic,"

00:48:50.860 --> 00:48:53.770
I think, twice, and definitely
read a number of his dialogues,

00:48:53.770 --> 00:48:57.220
to try to think deeply
about constitutionalism,

00:48:57.220 --> 00:49:00.220
politics, Republican government.

00:49:00.220 --> 00:49:02.170
And Utopia is, in
part, a product

00:49:02.170 --> 00:49:06.170
of a very sustained study
of his own of Plato.

00:49:06.170 --> 00:49:07.420
So you're totally right there.

00:49:07.420 --> 00:49:08.253
That's a good catch.

00:49:08.253 --> 00:49:10.150
And you're spot on.

00:49:10.150 --> 00:49:12.930
What do they want?

00:49:12.930 --> 00:49:16.000
I think More's got their
number in Raphael Hythloday.

00:49:16.000 --> 00:49:19.140
What he really wants is
everyone to praise him,

00:49:19.140 --> 00:49:21.300
everyone to say,
you're a genius,

00:49:21.300 --> 00:49:26.430
everyone to more or less put
him up, pay for him, and say,

00:49:26.430 --> 00:49:27.703
yeah, this guy's brilliant.

00:49:27.703 --> 00:49:28.620
He's got a great plan.

00:49:28.620 --> 00:49:32.790
Hopefully one day we can do it,
but let's give him some power.

00:49:32.790 --> 00:49:34.050
Let's have him rule--

00:49:34.050 --> 00:49:38.190
libido dominandi, the desire
to dominate other people.

00:49:38.190 --> 00:49:41.000
Gnosticism-- I've got
a secret teaching.

00:49:41.000 --> 00:49:42.750
It's really hard to
explain, and you don't

00:49:42.750 --> 00:49:43.833
understand-- it's science.

00:49:43.833 --> 00:49:44.730
It's history.

00:49:44.730 --> 00:49:46.290
It's complicated.

00:49:46.290 --> 00:49:48.000
Just give me all your
property, please.

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:49.800
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:49.800 --> 00:49:53.670
I really think it gets down to
those kinds of simple, really

00:49:53.670 --> 00:49:55.110
dangerous desires.

00:49:55.110 --> 00:50:01.470
But they're under a giant
just smokescreen, yes, of--

00:50:01.470 --> 00:50:05.480
but pink smoke, and green
smoke, and glitter, and bombs.

00:50:05.480 --> 00:50:07.410
And then they fall
for the image-- no,

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:10.230
I'm really doing
this good thing.

00:50:18.349 --> 00:50:20.266
SPEAKER 2: We have time
for one more question.

00:50:30.052 --> 00:50:31.760
AUDIENCE: I'll ask
you a simple question.

00:50:35.400 --> 00:50:39.000
Do you think socialism
has ever been achieved?

00:50:39.000 --> 00:50:42.980
It sounds to me it's a fantasy
and remains a fantasy, as far

00:50:42.980 --> 00:50:44.887
as any advanced society.

00:50:44.887 --> 00:50:46.970
MATTHEW MEHAN: Do I think
it's ever been achieved?

00:50:46.970 --> 00:50:47.870
AUDIENCE: Correct.

00:50:47.870 --> 00:50:48.680
MATTHEW MEHAN: No.

00:50:48.680 --> 00:50:49.630
No.

00:50:49.630 --> 00:50:54.110
If achievement is a
one-minute sentiment

00:50:54.110 --> 00:50:58.400
of a number of people with a
document in a room, then sure.

00:50:58.400 --> 00:51:01.190
I'm sure it's been
achieved a number of times.

00:51:01.190 --> 00:51:03.740
But no, we've never
had a working state

00:51:03.740 --> 00:51:07.910
that's actually been
socialistic in the promise.

00:51:07.910 --> 00:51:11.620
It's always been an
apparent socialism.

00:51:11.620 --> 00:51:13.370
That's the kind of
double vision you need.

00:51:16.020 --> 00:51:16.562
AUDIENCE: Oh.

00:51:16.562 --> 00:51:18.076
[INAUDIBLE] a retort?

00:51:18.076 --> 00:51:21.005
MATTHEW MEHAN: Oh, please.

00:51:21.005 --> 00:51:22.130
AUDIENCE: I agree with you.

00:51:22.130 --> 00:51:25.580
Because what happens,
there's always a small number

00:51:25.580 --> 00:51:28.970
controlling a massive number,
whether it's a monarchy

00:51:28.970 --> 00:51:30.620
or whatever.

00:51:30.620 --> 00:51:32.690
It seems to come down to that.

00:51:32.690 --> 00:51:36.345
And socialism is
another variety of that.

00:51:36.345 --> 00:51:37.220
MATTHEW MEHAN: Agree.

00:51:37.220 --> 00:51:39.080
Thank you.

00:51:39.080 --> 00:51:39.958
SPEAKER 2: Thank you.

00:51:39.958 --> 00:51:41.000
MATTHEW MEHAN: All right.

00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:44.050
[APPLAUSE]

